SACLA ANSI test crashes — GC-independent logic bugs and deep GC use-after-frees.

Found while resuming the SACLA ansi-tests (bootstrap/sacla/lisp/tests/).  Reduce a
crash to its triggering top-level form with BOCL_DBG_LOAD=1 (prints F0,F1,... as
each form is evaluated; the last F before the crash/hang is the trigger), then
classify it by loading the file with the GC disabled:

    ./sources/bocl --no-init --batch -on-error appease \
        --load bootstrap/sacla/lisp/tests/ansi-tests.lisp \
        -x '(cl::%gc-enable nil)' --load <FILE> -x '(cl::%gc-enable t)' \
        -x '(princ :done)'

A crash that PERSISTS with the GC off is a pure logic bug; one that DISAPPEARS
with the GC off is a GC use-after-free (rooting gap) that only fires under heap
pressure.  Diagnostic primitives (kernel.c): (cl::%gc-enable x), (cl::%gc-stress n),
(cl::%poison x) — the last toggles the use-after-free finder at runtime so it can
target a post-prelude workload without the finder's slow sweep over the prelude.

== FIXED (Slice 59) — aggregate #nA(...) array printing ==
Multidimensional / adjustable / displaced arrays (%md-array structs) printed as
the opaque #<%MD-ARRAY 0x…> instead of a readable aggregate.  Now:
  - prelude %MD-ARRAY-PRINT-STRING builds the #nA(...) form (rank>=2), #(...) for
    rank 1, #0A<elem> for rank 0, recursing row-major and printing each element
    with PRIN1 (escape) or PRINC; a CHARACTER %md-array prints as a string of its
    active elements.  Honours *PRINT-ARRAY* (NIL => return NIL => kernel prints
    #<…>) and, via PRIN1/PRINC, *PRINT-BASE*/*PRINT-ESCAPE*/*PRINT-CASE* on leaves.
  - printer.c print_md_array() (gated by structurep) calls it from both PRIN1 and
    PRINC just before the condition/CLOS fallback.
Verified: #2A/#3A nested, #0A scalar, *print-array* nil -> #<, base-2 element
formatting inside an array, all correct; no regression to list/vector/string/
bit-vector/ratio/symbol printing.
STILL OPEN for arrays: *PRINT-LEVEL*/*PRINT-LENGTH* truncation (# / ...) and
*PRINT-CIRCLE* (#n=/#n#) inside arrays (must-printer F370/F371 use :level/:length,
F389-394 use :circle) — and the F45 fill-pointer KERNEL-STRING (not a %md-array)
still prints all elements.

== FIXED (Slice 58) — missing pretty-printer control variables ==
*PRINT-LINES*, *PRINT-RIGHT-MARGIN*, *PRINT-MISER-WIDTH* were exported from CL
(cl-exports.lisp) but never DEFVAR'd, so referencing them was an unbound-variable
error (must-printer F10/F11).  Added the three defvars (default NIL per CLHS).

== FIXED (Slice 57) — *PRINT-CASE* + *PRINT-GENSYM* (symbol printing) ==
  - *PRINT-CASE* (:UPCASE/:DOWNCASE/:CAPITALIZE): print_name_escaped() now folds
    the case of UNESCAPED symbol/keyword/package names (escaped |…| names stay
    verbatim per CLHS 22.1.3.3.1).  Default :UPCASE is a verbatim no-op, so normal
    output is unchanged; :DOWNCASE -> "foo", :CAPITALIZE -> "Foo-Bar".  Assumes the
    standard readtable-case :UPCASE (full readtable-case-aware printing — needed
    for must-printer F203/204/205/233, which sweep readtable-case x *print-case* —
    is still open; those are NOT regressions, they fail today too).
  - *PRINT-GENSYM* (default T): the #: prefix on an uninterned symbol is now
    suppressed when *PRINT-GENSYM* is NIL (must-printer F225:
    (write-to-string (make-symbol "G01") :gensym nil :escape t) => "G01").
Verified 10/10 targeted (downcase/capitalize, keyword, escaped-stays-verbatim,
gensym on/off, round-trip preserved) with no change to default printing.

== FIXED (Slice 56) — *PRINT-BASE* / *PRINT-RADIX* radix printing (~80 forms) ==
The kernel printer ignored *PRINT-BASE* and *PRINT-RADIX*: integers, bignums, and
ratios always printed in decimal with no radix prefix (must-printer F51-F125,
F89-F125 and the F85 base-2..36 loop).  PRIN1 and PRINC now honour both, at every
integer/ratio LEAF (so nested numbers in lists/vectors get the radix too):
  - printer.c: print_integer_radix() / print_ratio_radix() consult *PRINT-BASE*
    (2..36) and *PRINT-RADIX*, emit the prefix (#b/#o/#x, or #NNr for other
    bases), the digits (uppercase A-Z), and — for an INTEGER in base 10 with
    *PRINT-RADIX* — the trailing "." marker (a ratio in base 10 uses "#10r"
    instead).  fmt_write_integer handles fixnums; bignums go through ...
  - kernel.c: print_bignum_radix() — radix conversion of the base-10^9 magnitude
    limbs by repeated short division by BASE (remainder*10^9+limb fits a uword
    for BASE<=36).  Base 10 still uses the faster print_bignum.
  - prelude write-to-string lost its non-decimal "number-to-string" shortcut
    (which bypassed the kernel printer and dropped the radix prefix); everything
    now routes through WRITE, which binds *PRINT-BASE*/*PRINT-RADIX*.
  - Bonus: the ratio branch used integer_value() on numerator/denominator, so a
    ratio with a BIGNUM part printed garbage; it now uses the bignum-safe path.
Verified: 27/28 targeted cases pass (base 2/8/10/16/24/36, integer/ratio/bignum,
radix on/off, nested-in-list, princ path, base-10 bignum trailing dot); FORMAT
~D/~X/~B and *PRINT-BASE*-vs-~D isolation unaffected.  The 1 remaining is a READER
gap, not a printer gap: the ratio literal 2/<bignum> truncates its bignum
denominator to a word on READ (separate bignum-ratio support, deferred-bignum-ops)
— the printer already handles bignum ratios correctly once one is constructed.

== FIXED (Slice 55) — readable symbol printing + #: reader (pure logic bugs) ==
The must-printer F145 SECONDARY correctness bug (and a whole class with it):
PRIN1 / WRITE :readably / WRITE :escape printed a symbol's name RAW
(princ(symbol_name)), so any symbol whose name would not read back unchanged was
emitted unreadably:
  - a name with #\\ , #\| , whitespace, or other special/terminating chars
    (e.g. the |\\| symbol printed a bare "\\");
  - a lowercase or mixed-case name (default :UPCASE would fold it on read-back);
  - a numeric-looking name ("123", "1/2", "1.5") that would read as a number;
  - the empty name and all-dots names ("." "...").
Fix (printer.c): prin1_inner now escapes both the package-prefix and the symbol
name via print_name_escaped() — symbol_name_needs_escape() decides, and when
escaping is needed the whole name is wrapped in |...| with internal | and \\
backslash-escaped (vertical-bar quoting preserves case + every constituent
verbatim, so it is always a correct readable form).  PRINC is unchanged (still
raw/aesthetic).  Normal uppercase symbols, keywords, and lists print exactly as
before (no spurious escaping).

Uncovered + fixed en route (reader.c): the #:NAME (uninterned symbol) reader used
the coarse constituentp(), which classifies #\# as NON-constituent, so it stopped
the name at the first '#': "#:FOO#BAR" read back as FOO, not FOO#BAR.  (The normal
token reader was already correct — it uses the readtable-based token_continues(),
which continues across non-terminating macro chars like #.)  The #: loop now uses
token_continues() too, matching parse_token.

Verified: make-symbol round-trips for {"\\","foo","FOO","123","1/2","1.5","",
"a b","FOO#BAR","#FOO",".","...","nil","Mixed"} all read back to an equal name
(STRING= name (symbol-name (read-from-string (write-to-string sym :readably t)))).
The F145 round-trip (symbol whose name is a single #\\) now reads back to a symbol
named "\\" instead of erroring.  NOTE: the must-printer F145 HANG is the separate,
still-open deep GC use-after-free below; only its correctness half is fixed here.

== FIXED (Slice 77) ==
  - must-symbol SEGV  -> AREF/AREF-SET on a STRING crashed (VECTOR_Class tested
    before STRING_Class; a string IS a vector, so it went through svref and read
    octet bytes as Refs).  Reordered the type checks.  must-symbol loads clean.
  - must-do HANG      -> DO stepped variables SEQUENTIALLY (DO* semantics) instead
    of in parallel, breaking (do ((i ..(1+ i))(j .. i)) ..) and in-place NREVERSE.
    DO now binds each step into a temp then assigns.  must-do loads clean.
  - must-cons HANG #1 -> LIST-LENGTH looped forever on a circular list; now uses
    Floyd's tortoise/hare (NIL for circular, type-error for dotted).

== STILL OPEN: deep GC use-after-frees (DISAPPEAR with the GC off) ==
Two context-dependent hangs that COMPLETE with (cl::%gc-enable nil) but hang under
normal GC once the file's heap has grown — i.e. a rooting gap whose freed cons is
reused (in a destructive op) or whose dangling ref is followed, only when a GC
happens to fire mid-operation (never in isolation, where the heap is small):

  - must-cons F589: (set-exclusive-or (intersection l1 l2 :test #'char= :key ..)
    '("B" "F" "h") :test #'char= :key ..).  intersection/remove-if/find/set-
    exclusive-or are all Lisp (interpreter-rooted), so the gap is in a C primitive
    they reach.  Faults as SIGBUS under (%gc-stress 15) or hangs (cycle) under
    natural GC.
  - must-printer F145: (symbol-name (read-from-string
    (write-to-string '|\\| :readably t))).  Backslash-named symbol round-trip;
    loads clean in isolation, hangs only in the accumulated must-printer context.
    (Secondary correctness bug: write-to-string :readably of that symbol emits a
    bare "\" instead of "\\"/"|\|", so the round-trip would error even if it did
    not hang.)

Why the poison finder has not localized them yet:
  - GCSTRESS=1+poison sweeps the WHOLE heap on every allocation -> too slow to
    reach/complete a post-prelude form; moderate stress either misses the window
    or the gap SIGBUSes via raw pointer arithmetic before the instrumented
    accessors run.
  - In poison mode the freed cons is QUARANTINED (not reused), so the reuse-cycle
    never forms; cons_car/cons_cdr/cons_rplaca/cons_rplacd now report a poisoned
    cons (Slice 78), but the faulting access here is inside a C primitive's own
    struct/pointer arithmetic, not these accessors.
  - bocl's custom zone allocator is opaque to AddressSanitizer (the zones are one
    big malloc block), so an ASan build would not catch a use-after-free WITHIN a
    zone.  sample(1) / lldb attach need elevated permissions in this environment.

Next ideas: (a) a "stop the world and verify_no_dangling after every Nth GC under
moderate stress" mode that names the C frame that allocated the now-freed block;
(b) shrink DefaultZoneSize so natural GC fires far sooner, making F589 reproduce
in a near-isolation context that the finder CAN complete; (c) audit the C
sequence/predicate primitives reachable from find/remove-if (eql, the :key/:test
funcall trampoline) for an unrooted cons held across the predicate call.

== Debugging attempt (payload-poison) — abandoned; lessons ==
Tried turning the hang into a locatable fault by (1) filling each freed block's
payload with 0x0505050505050505 so a RAW dangling deref faults at a recognizable
address, and (2) a (%poison t) runtime toggle to enable the finder only for a
targeted post-prelude form.  Both produced FALSE faults and were reverted /
fixed-but-still-limited:
  - Payload fill is UNSOUND for this allocator: a constructor that reuses an
    evicted block without initializing every slot then reads the stale 0x0505 as a
    pointer — a fault that is NOT a use-after-free.  Reverted (poison_block leaves
    the payload intact again).
  - The (%poison t) toggle set gc_poison_mode without allocating the quarantine
    ring (calloc'd only for env BOCL_POISON in memory_initialize) -> poison_block
    wrote a NULL ring -> crash.  FIXED (set_gc_poison now calls poison_ring_ensure;
    BOCL_POISON_BT gates the slow per-free backtrace).
  - Even with the ring allocated, the toggle + heavy post-prelude consing under
    GCSTRESS faults in allocate: the quarantine EVICTION path (release oldest ->
    objcount=FREE_BLOCK) corrupts the free list once the ring (262144) fills.  The
    env finder never hit this because GCSTRESS=1 aborts on the first gap long
    before the ring fills.  So the poison finder is reliable ONLY for early gaps
    that abort before the ring fills; for heavy/late workloads it is not.
Net: F589/F145 remain UNLOCALIZED.  The reliable path is a guard-page debug
allocator (one mprotect'd region per object) — real infrastructure, and
incompatible with the packed zone allocator without a rewrite.

== Real gap fixed en route ==
obarray_add_symbol (kernel.c) — the package-obarray twin of symbol_intern_obarray
(Slice 76): obarray + sym were not rooted across the cons + sym_hash_add when
adding a NEW symbol.  Fixed with the same cold-not-found-branch rooting.

== Small missing features — DONE (Slices 80-81) ==
must-symbol now loads with ZERO skipped forms.  Implemented:
  - GENSYM accepts an INTEGER suffix designator and uses the standard
    *GENSYM-COUNTER* special variable (kernel-side, sources/sequences.c).
  - SET and FDEFINITION (prelude.lisp).
  - (setf (get sym ind default) val) — the optional default is now evaluated for
    effect and ignored, instead of erroring "Too many arguments, unused: (TMP)".
